<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Persepolis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Persepolis was the ceremonial capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, located in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. Subject nations presented tribute there, as depicted in the reliefs of its palace complex. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> occupied it and burned the palace in 330 BCE, and its administrative tablets show the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a>'s bureaucracy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persepolis.jpg" alt="Persepolis" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurens R. Krol · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018-09-21_Iran,_Persepolis,_Tachara_(from_the_southeast).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Persepolis stands in the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marvdasht">Marvdasht</a>, encircled by the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars province</a>, near the small river Pulvar, which flows into the Kur River. The site includes a terrace of 125,000 square metres, partly artificial and partly cut from a mountain, with its east side resting against Rahmat Mountain. Its exact function remains unclear.</p><p>It was not one of the largest cities of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> but appears to have been a grand ceremonial complex occupied only seasonally, raised on a walled platform with five palaces or halls of varying size. Until recently most archaeologists held that it was used chiefly to celebrate Nowruz, the Persian New Year, when the Iranian nobility and tributary peoples brought gifts to the king, as the stairway reliefs show.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest remains date to 515 BCE. The French archaeologist André Godard, who excavated the site in the early 1930s, believed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> chose it but that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> built the terrace and the palaces, which inscriptions on the buildings support. The country's true capitals were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, which may be why the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> did not know the city until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took and plundered it. Darius built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apadana">Apadana</a>, the largest palace, for official audiences; its foundation tablets of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> bear his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> inscription.</p><p>After invading Achaemenid Persia in 330 BCE, Alexander sent the main force of his army to Persepolis by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-road">Royal Road</a> and allowed his troops to loot the city. Soon afterwards the wooden parts of the complex were destroyed by fire. According to one theory Alexander set the fire deliberately to avenge the Persian destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>; according to another, he did so at the urging of the courtesan Thaïs during a feast, while drunk. In 316 BCE Persepolis was still the capital of Persia as a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>, but it gradually declined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Persepolis is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and one of the key cultural heritage sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The ruins of colossal buildings of dark-grey marble stand on the terrace, with fifteen pillars intact and three more re-erected since 1970; the Tachara Palace is the most intact building, having escaped the burning. García de Silva Figueroa in 1618 was the first Western traveller to identify the site known in Iran as Chehel Minar with the Persepolis of the classical authors. Study of the trilingual royal inscriptions found there led to the initial decipherment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> in the early 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Persepolis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Persepolis</b> was the ceremonial capital where the empire's subject nations presented tribute, Susa the principal administrative centre and Ecbatana the summer residence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Achaemenids some Saka lands were attached to the eastern satrapies, and the reliefs of the Apadana at <b>Persepolis</b> show Saka delegations in pointed hats bringing horses, garments and bracelets to the king; but Persian control beyond the Syr Darya was probably loose and based on alliance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2011 a joint Italian-Iranian team found at Tol-e Ajori, near the Achaemenid capital of <b>Persepolis</b>, a monumental gate with similar plan and decoration, with glazed brick fragments showing the same aurochs and mušḫuššu motifs, dated to the mid-to-late sixth century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>His court moved between <b>Persepolis</b>, Susa, Ecbatana and Babylon, and the reliefs of the Apadana at Persepolis show delegations of the subject peoples bringing him gifts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory broke Achaemenid power and opened the road to Babylon, Susa and <b>Persepolis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-1">1 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After Alexander's conquest Persis became part of the Seleucid Empire, but in the 3rd century BCE local dynasts called frataraka ('governor' or 'leader') began issuing their own coins from Istakhr and <b>Persepolis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-persis">Kingdom of Persis</a></li></ul></section>
